<urn:ontoweee:analysis:profile:source-9f8e67169119c4a8%2Ffraction-2> a analysis:SampleProfile ;
    analysis:archiveEntityId "Component__1_mm__Ni_Cd_" ;
    analysis:case_id "source-9f8e67169119c4a8" ;
    analysis:chemistry "ni-cd" ;
    analysis:chemistry_resolved true ;
    analysis:evidenceSpan "Each selected battery type was fed into a continuous milling machine for size reduction, and a fractionation process was then applied, separating the materials in a coarse fraction >3 mm (including the plastic of external shell, residual plastic pieces and felt and cardboard from internal battery protection), and a fine fraction <3 mm (mainly made up of metallic powder deriving from electrodes).",
        "In the laboratory, after a magnetic separation of the coarse ferrous residues contained in Ni–Cd batteries, all samples were divided through a suitable sieve into two further size fractions: U < 1 mm and 1 mm < U < 3 mm. In this way, a possible difference in metal concentration in the various fractions was investigated." ;
    analysis:feedstock_history "end-of-life" ;
    analysis:id "source-9f8e67169119c4a8/fraction-2" ;
    analysis:label "Ni-Cd fraction below 1 mm" ;
    analysis:material_class "mixed-electrode-material" ;
    analysis:node_id "http://weee-kg.org/source:Chemical%20Characterisation%20of%20Spent%20Rechargeable%20Batteries:Component__1_mm__Ni_Cd_" ;
    analysis:note "Table 3 reports leachate release in mg/kg and is excluded from feedstock composition." ;
    analysis:origin "collection-boxes" ;
    analysis:paper "Chemical Characterisation of Spent Rechargeable Batteries" ;
    analysis:payload "{\"id\": \"source-9f8e67169119c4a8/fraction-2\", \"label\": \"Ni-Cd fraction below 1 mm\", \"chemistry\": \"ni-cd\", \"stream\": \"separated-fractions\", \"origin\": \"collection-boxes\", \"state\": \"Milled, fractionated and dried to constant mass at 105 °C\", \"entity_ids\": [\"Component__1_mm__Ni_Cd_\"], \"method_family\": null, \"note\": \"Table 3 reports leachate release in mg/kg and is excluded from feedstock composition.\", \"evidence\": [\"Each selected battery type was fed into a continuous milling machine for size reduction, and a fractionation process was then applied, separating the materials in a coarse fraction >3 mm (including the plastic of external shell, residual plastic pieces and felt and cardboard from internal battery protection), and a fine fraction <3 mm (mainly made up of metallic powder deriving from electrodes).\", \"In the laboratory, after a magnetic separation of the coarse ferrous residues contained in Ni–Cd batteries, all samples were divided through a suitable sieve into two further size fractions: U < 1 mm and 1 mm < U < 3 mm. In this way, a possible difference in metal concentration in the various fractions was investigated.\"], \"material_class\": \"mixed-electrode-material\", \"preparation\": [\"milled\", \"screened\", \"oven-dried\"], \"size_fraction_mm\": [null, 1], \"thermal_treatment\": null, \"feedstock_history\": \"end-of-life\", \"chemistry_resolved\": true, \"classification_evidence\": [\"Each selected battery type was fed into a continuous milling machine for size reduction, and a fractionation process was then applied, separating the materials in a coarse fraction >3 mm (including the plastic of external shell, residual plastic pieces and felt and cardboard from internal battery protection), and a fine fraction <3 mm (mainly made up of metallic powder deriving from electrodes).\", \"In the laboratory, after a magnetic separation of the coarse ferrous residues contained in Ni–Cd batteries, all samples were divided through a suitable sieve into two further size fractions: U < 1 mm and 1 mm < U < 3 mm. In this way, a possible difference in metal concentration in the various fractions was investigated.\", \"All metals except mercury were measured by ICP/AES; mercury was analysed by cold vapour atomic absorption spectrometry (CVAAS).\", \"Weight loss, mainly due to the vaporisation of moisture and other volatile compounds, was determined by drying the samples to a constant mass at 105 \\u0003C (about 3–4 h were needed). All data reported refer to dry weight.\"], \"case_id\": \"source-9f8e67169119c4a8\", \"paper\": \"Chemical Characterisation of Spent Rechargeable Batteries\", \"node_id\": \"http://weee-kg.org/source:Chemical%20Characterisation%20of%20Spent%20Rechargeable%20Batteries:Component__1_mm__Ni_Cd_\"}" ;
    analysis:state "Milled, fractionated and dried to constant mass at 105 °C" ;
    analysis:stream "separated-fractions" .

<urn:ontoweee:analysis:profile:nimh-recovery%2Fnegative> a analysis:SampleProfile ;
    analysis:archiveEntityId "Component_negative_electrode" ;
    analysis:case_id "nimh-recovery" ;
    analysis:chemistry "ni-mh" ;
    analysis:chemistry_resolved true ;
    analysis:evidenceSpan "The Ni-MH batteries used in this work were cylinder-shaped batteries TOSHIBAŽ TH-3A type. The commercial extractants employed, bis 2-ethylhexyl phosphoric acid. Ž. Ž. Ž. D2EHPA was kindly donated by Daihachi Chemical Industry and trioctylamine TOA was purchased from Aldrich Chemical Company Inc. These reagents were utilized without any further purification. The concentrations of the extractants in the organic solution were 25% by volume. The diluent was kerosene. 1-Dodecanol was used as third phase inhibitor in the TOA system, and its concentration was 15 vol.%. The organic solution of TOA was pre-equilibrated by contacting with twice volume of 2 M hydrochloric acid solution for 10 min before the extraction experiments. Other reagents were of analytical reagent grade quality." ;
    analysis:feedstock_history "end-of-life" ;
    analysis:id "nimh-recovery/negative" ;
    analysis:label "Ni-MH negative electrode" ;
    analysis:material_class "negative-electrode" ;
    analysis:node_id "http://weee-kg.org/source:Hydrometallurgical%20Process%20for%20Recovery%20of%20Metal%20Values%20from%20Spent%20Nickel-Metal%20Hydride%20Secondary%20Batteries:Component_negative_electrode" ;
    analysis:note "Original table reports electrode chemical components in wt.%; oxygen and unreported constituents are not reconstructed. Do not treat these as whole-cell composition." ;
    analysis:origin "spent-batteries" ;
    analysis:paper "Hydrometallurgical Process for Recovery of Metal Values from Spent Nickel-Metal Hydride Secondary Batteries" ;
    analysis:payload "{\"id\": \"nimh-recovery/negative\", \"label\": \"Ni-MH negative electrode\", \"chemistry\": \"ni-mh\", \"stream\": \"anode\", \"origin\": \"spent-batteries\", \"state\": \"Separated electrode; reported chemical components\", \"entity_ids\": [\"Component_negative_electrode\"], \"method_family\": null, \"note\": \"Original table reports electrode chemical components in wt.%; oxygen and unreported constituents are not reconstructed. Do not treat these as whole-cell composition.\", \"evidence\": [\"The Ni-MH batteries used in this work were cylinder-shaped batteries TOSHIBAŽ TH-3A type. The commercial extractants employed, bis 2-ethylhexyl phosphoric acid. Ž. Ž. Ž. D2EHPA was kindly donated by Daihachi Chemical Industry and trioctylamine TOA was purchased from Aldrich Chemical Company Inc. These reagents were utilized without any further purification. The concentrations of the extractants in the organic solution were 25% by volume. The diluent was kerosene. 1-Dodecanol was used as third phase inhibitor in the TOA system, and its concentration was 15 vol.%. The organic solution of TOA was pre-equilibrated by contacting with twice volume of 2 M hydrochloric acid solution for 10 min before the extraction experiments. Other reagents were of analytical reagent grade quality.\"], \"material_class\": \"negative-electrode\", \"preparation\": [\"dismantled\", \"separated\"], \"size_fraction_mm\": null, \"thermal_treatment\": null, \"feedstock_history\": \"end-of-life\", \"chemistry_resolved\": true, \"classification_evidence\": [\"The Ni-MH batteries used in this work were cylinder-shaped batteries TOSHIBAŽ TH-3A type. The commercial extractants employed, bis 2-ethylhexyl phosphoric acid. Ž. Ž. Ž. D2EHPA was kindly donated by Daihachi Chemical Industry and trioctylamine TOA was purchased from Aldrich Chemical Company Inc. These reagents were utilized without any further purification. The concentrations of the extractants in the organic solution were 25% by volume. The diluent was kerosene. 1-Dodecanol was used as third phase inhibitor in the TOA system, and its concentration was 15 vol.%. The organic solution of TOA was pre-equilibrated by contacting with twice volume of 2 M hydrochloric acid solution for 10 min before the extraction experiments. Other reagents were of analytical reagent grade quality.\", \"The nickel-metal hydride batteries were cut in half longitudinally to separate the metallic cases from the internal battery rolls which consisted of the positive and negative electrodes plus a nylon separator. The cells weighed an average of 24.8 g each and contained an AB5 - t y p e rare earth metal system negative electrode. The weight of theŽ. positive electrode in one cell was 7.27 g and that of the negative electrode was 10.57 g in which the alloy powder accounted for 87.6% and the metal-mesh 12.4%. The components of the active material on the positive electrode, the alloy powder and the metal-mesh on the negative electrode were determined separately by total dissolution with aqua regia.\"], \"case_id\": \"nimh-recovery\", \"paper\": \"Hydrometallurgical Process for Recovery of Metal Values from Spent Nickel-Metal Hydride Secondary Batteries\", \"node_id\": \"http://weee-kg.org/source:Hydrometallurgical%20Process%20for%20Recovery%20of%20Metal%20Values%20from%20Spent%20Nickel-Metal%20Hydride%20Secondary%20Batteries:Component_negative_electrode\"}" ;
    analysis:state "Separated electrode; reported chemical components" ;
    analysis:stream "anode" .

<urn:ontoweee:analysis:profile:source-0b9440848ba21f0c%2Felectrodes> a analysis:SampleProfile ;
    analysis:archiveEntityId "Component_electrode",
        "Material_mixed_electrode_material" ;
    analysis:case_id "source-0b9440848ba21f0c" ;
    analysis:chemistry "lco" ;
    analysis:chemistry_resolved true ;
    analysis:evidenceSpan "In order to avoid short-circuit and self-ignition, 100 pieces of spent LIBs with the same type were discharged completely in 5 wt% sodium chloride solution for 48 h and dried naturally for 24 h afterwards. All batteries were manually disassembled into metallic shell, cathode strips, anode strips and organic separators. Then the cathode strips and anode strips were placed in the impact crusher with the number ratio 1:1. After crushing and screening, the fine powder under 0.074 mm was mixed electrode materials (LiCoO2 and graphite). The content of LiCoO2 in mixed electrode powder was measured by the combination of X-ray fluorescence (XRF, Bruker S8 Tiger, Germany) and X-ray diffraction (XRD, Bruker D8 Advance, Germany). Results indicated that LiCoO2 accounted for 66.75% of the mixed electrode powder." ;
    analysis:feedstock_history "end-of-life" ;
    analysis:id "source-0b9440848ba21f0c/electrodes" ;
    analysis:label "LiCoO2 / graphite electrode powder" ;
    analysis:material_class "mixed-electrode-material" ;
    analysis:method_family "Flotation" ;
    analysis:node_id "http://weee-kg.org/source:A%20Promising%20Physical%20Method%20for%20Recovery%20of%20Licoo%202%20and%20Graphite%20from%20Spent%20Lithium-Ion%20Batteries%20Grinding%20Flotation:Component_electrode" ;
    analysis:note "66.75% describes LiCoO2 compound content. XPS surface percentages are excluded from elemental mass summaries." ;
    analysis:origin "spent-batteries" ;
    analysis:paper "A Promising Physical Method for Recovery of Licoo 2 and Graphite from Spent Lithium-Ion Batteries Grinding Flotation" ;
    analysis:payload "{\"id\": \"source-0b9440848ba21f0c/electrodes\", \"label\": \"LiCoO2 / graphite electrode powder\", \"chemistry\": \"lco\", \"stream\": \"mixed-electrodes\", \"origin\": \"spent-batteries\", \"state\": \"Crushed and screened below 0.074 mm\", \"entity_ids\": [\"Component_electrode\", \"Material_mixed_electrode_material\"], \"method_family\": \"Flotation\", \"note\": \"66.75% describes LiCoO2 compound content. XPS surface percentages are excluded from elemental mass summaries.\", \"evidence\": [\"In order to avoid short-circuit and self-ignition, 100 pieces of spent LIBs with the same type were discharged completely in 5 wt% sodium chloride solution for 48 h and dried naturally for 24 h afterwards. All batteries were manually disassembled into metallic shell, cathode strips, anode strips and organic separators. Then the cathode strips and anode strips were placed in the impact crusher with the number ratio 1:1. After crushing and screening, the fine powder under 0.074 mm was mixed electrode materials (LiCoO2 and graphite). The content of LiCoO2 in mixed electrode powder was measured by the combination of X-ray fluorescence (XRF, Bruker S8 Tiger, Germany) and X-ray diffraction (XRD, Bruker D8 Advance, Germany). Results indicated that LiCoO2 accounted for 66.75% of the mixed electrode powder.\"], \"material_class\": \"mixed-electrode-material\", \"preparation\": [\"discharged\", \"dismantled\", \"crushed\", \"screened\"], \"size_fraction_mm\": [null, 0.074], \"thermal_treatment\": null, \"feedstock_history\": \"end-of-life\", \"chemistry_resolved\": true, \"classification_evidence\": [\"In order to avoid short-circuit and self-ignition, 100 pieces of spent LIBs with the same type were discharged completely in 5 wt% sodium chloride solution for 48 h and dried naturally for 24 h afterwards. All batteries were manually disassembled into metallic shell, cathode strips, anode strips and organic separators. Then the cathode strips and anode strips were placed in the impact crusher with the number ratio 1:1. After crushing and screening, the fine powder under 0.074 mm was mixed electrode materials (LiCoO2 and graphite). The content of LiCoO2 in mixed electrode powder was measured by the combination of X-ray fluorescence (XRF, Bruker S8 Tiger, Germany) and X-ray diffraction (XRD, Bruker D8 Advance, Germany). Results indicated that LiCoO2 accounted for 66.75% of the mixed electrode powder.\"], \"case_id\": \"source-0b9440848ba21f0c\", \"paper\": \"A Promising Physical Method for Recovery of Licoo 2 and Graphite from Spent Lithium-Ion Batteries Grinding Flotation\", \"node_id\": \"http://weee-kg.org/source:A%20Promising%20Physical%20Method%20for%20Recovery%20of%20Licoo%202%20and%20Graphite%20from%20Spent%20Lithium-Ion%20Batteries%20Grinding%20Flotation:Component_electrode\"}" ;
    analysis:state "Crushed and screened below 0.074 mm" ;
    analysis:stream "mixed-electrodes" .

