<urn:ontoweee:analysis:profile:anode-leaching%2Fanode> a analysis:SampleProfile ;
    analysis:archiveEntityId "Component_anode_active_material" ;
    analysis:case_id "anode-leaching" ;
    analysis:chemistry "li-ion-unspecified" ;
    analysis:chemistry_resolved false ;
    analysis:evidenceSpan "Procedures of the present work are illustrated in Fig. 1 (a) and (b). The spent LIBs were discharged firstly to prevent the dangers of short-circuits and spontaneous combustion. Then they were dismantled manually to different parts: cathode, anode, organic separator and the plastic case. With aid of scraper and tweezers, active materials were separated from the anode. These partitioned black active materials were placed in a furnace and calcined at 500 ^∘C for one hour to remove the organic components.",
        "Table 1 shows the metal compositions of the anode active materials. From the table, it can be noted that except tiny amounts of Co, Ni and Fe, the main metals contained in the powder materials are Cu and Li. Especially Li, its content is 30.07 mg/g, much higher than that of the environmental standards. The high content of lithium maybe come from two aspects." ;
    analysis:feedstock_history "end-of-life" ;
    analysis:id "anode-leaching/anode" ;
    analysis:label "Calcined anode active material" ;
    analysis:material_class "anode-active-material" ;
    analysis:method_family "Acid leaching" ;
    analysis:node_id "http://weee-kg.org/source:Leaching%20Lithium%20from%20the%20Anode%20Electrode%20Materials%20of%20Spent%20Lithium-Ion%20Batteries%20by%20Hydrochloric%20Acid%20%28Hcl%29:Component_anode_active_material" ;
    analysis:note "The source gives 500 °C in preparation and 600 °C in characterization. This overview retains the broader calcined state." ;
    analysis:origin "spent-batteries" ;
    analysis:paper "Leaching Lithium from the Anode Electrode Materials of Spent Lithium-Ion Batteries by Hydrochloric Acid (Hcl)" ;
    analysis:payload "{\"id\": \"anode-leaching/anode\", \"label\": \"Calcined anode active material\", \"chemistry\": \"li-ion-unspecified\", \"stream\": \"anode\", \"origin\": \"spent-batteries\", \"state\": \"Separated and calcined anode material\", \"entity_ids\": [\"Component_anode_active_material\"], \"method_family\": \"Acid leaching\", \"note\": \"The source gives 500 °C in preparation and 600 °C in characterization. This overview retains the broader calcined state.\", \"evidence\": [\"Procedures of the present work are illustrated in Fig. 1 (a) and (b). The spent LIBs were discharged firstly to prevent the dangers of short-circuits and spontaneous combustion. Then they were dismantled manually to different parts: cathode, anode, organic separator and the plastic case. With aid of scraper and tweezers, active materials were separated from the anode. These partitioned black active materials were placed in a furnace and calcined at 500 ^∘C for one hour to remove the organic components.\", \"Table 1 shows the metal compositions of the anode active materials. From the table, it can be noted that except tiny amounts of Co, Ni and Fe, the main metals contained in the powder materials are Cu and Li. Especially Li, its content is 30.07 mg/g, much higher than that of the environmental standards. The high content of lithium maybe come from two aspects.\"], \"material_class\": \"anode-active-material\", \"preparation\": [\"dismantled\", \"scraped\", \"calcined\"], \"size_fraction_mm\": null, \"thermal_treatment\": {\"temperature_c\": 500, \"time_min\": 60}, \"feedstock_history\": \"end-of-life\", \"chemistry_resolved\": false, \"classification_evidence\": [\"Procedures of the present work are illustrated in Fig. 1 (a) and (b). The spent LIBs were discharged firstly to prevent the dangers of short-circuits and spontaneous combustion. Then they were dismantled manually to different parts: cathode, anode, organic separator and the plastic case. With aid of scraper and tweezers, active materials were separated from the anode. These partitioned black active materials were placed in a furnace and calcined at 500 ^∘C for one hour to remove the organic components.\", \"Table 1 shows the metal compositions of the anode active materials. From the table, it can be noted that except tiny amounts of Co, Ni and Fe, the main metals contained in the powder materials are Cu and Li. Especially Li, its content is 30.07 mg/g, much higher than that of the environmental standards. The high content of lithium maybe come from two aspects.\"], \"case_id\": \"anode-leaching\", \"paper\": \"Leaching Lithium from the Anode Electrode Materials of Spent Lithium-Ion Batteries by Hydrochloric Acid (Hcl)\", \"node_id\": \"http://weee-kg.org/source:Leaching%20Lithium%20from%20the%20Anode%20Electrode%20Materials%20of%20Spent%20Lithium-Ion%20Batteries%20by%20Hydrochloric%20Acid%20%28Hcl%29:Component_anode_active_material\"}" ;
    analysis:state "Separated and calcined anode material" ;
    analysis:stream "anode" .

<urn:ontoweee:analysis:profile:source-3bb6879d7ff610d4%2Fcathode> a analysis:SampleProfile ;
    analysis:archiveEntityId "Component_cathodic_material" ;
    analysis:case_id "source-3bb6879d7ff610d4" ;
    analysis:chemistry "mixed-li-ion" ;
    analysis:chemistry_resolved false ;
    analysis:evidenceSpan "Materials and Reagents. Spent LIBs from different consumer electronics, including mobile phones, cameras, and portable power storage devices, were collected for this study. The total weight of the samples was 1 kg. Aqua regia (volume ratio of HCl to H V̅O3 = 3: 1) was used to dissolve the cathodic materials fully in order to analyze their metal contents. L-Tartaric acid was employed as the leachant and hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) as the reductant. All the solutions were prepared in deionized water, and all the reagents were of analytical grade.",
        "NCM materials of varying proportions have similar XRD patterns. Moreover, the intensity of peaks in the residues after leaching is clearly weaker than that in the cathodic materials. The content of the metals present in the cathodic materials is shown in Table S1. The metals in the cathodic materials included Li (6.28 wt%), Ni (11.85 wt%), Co (35.52 wt%), Mn (8.15 wt%), and Al (0.11 wt%). The existence of a slight amount of Al is from the already decayed cathode, which was easily shredded during the ultrasonic cleaning." ;
    analysis:feedstock_history "end-of-life" ;
    analysis:id "source-3bb6879d7ff610d4/cathode" ;
    analysis:label "Cathodic material from mixed consumer LIBs" ;
    analysis:material_class "cathode-active-material" ;
    analysis:method_family "Organic-acid leaching" ;
    analysis:node_id "http://weee-kg.org/source:Recovery%20of%20Lithium%2C%20Nickel%2C%20Cobalt%2C%20and%20Manganese%20from%20Spent%20Lithium-Ion%20Batteries%20Using%20Scplscp-Tartaric%20Acid%20as%20a%20Leachant:Component_cathodic_material" ;
    analysis:note "" ;
    analysis:origin "consumer-electronics" ;
    analysis:paper "Recovery of Lithium, Nickel, Cobalt, and Manganese from Spent Lithium-Ion Batteries Using Scplscp-Tartaric Acid as a Leachant" ;
    analysis:payload "{\"id\": \"source-3bb6879d7ff610d4/cathode\", \"label\": \"Cathodic material from mixed consumer LIBs\", \"chemistry\": \"mixed-li-ion\", \"stream\": \"cathode\", \"origin\": \"consumer-electronics\", \"state\": \"Ultrasonically separated cathodic material\", \"entity_ids\": [\"Component_cathodic_material\"], \"method_family\": \"Organic-acid leaching\", \"note\": \"\", \"evidence\": [\"Materials and Reagents. Spent LIBs from different consumer electronics, including mobile phones, cameras, and portable power storage devices, were collected for this study. The total weight of the samples was 1 kg. Aqua regia (volume ratio of HCl to H V̅O3 = 3: 1) was used to dissolve the cathodic materials fully in order to analyze their metal contents. L-Tartaric acid was employed as the leachant and hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) as the reductant. All the solutions were prepared in deionized water, and all the reagents were of analytical grade.\", \"NCM materials of varying proportions have similar XRD patterns. Moreover, the intensity of peaks in the residues after leaching is clearly weaker than that in the cathodic materials. The content of the metals present in the cathodic materials is shown in Table S1. The metals in the cathodic materials included Li (6.28 wt%), Ni (11.85 wt%), Co (35.52 wt%), Mn (8.15 wt%), and Al (0.11 wt%). The existence of a slight amount of Al is from the already decayed cathode, which was easily shredded during the ultrasonic cleaning.\"], \"material_class\": \"cathode-active-material\", \"preparation\": [\"ultrasonically-separated\"], \"size_fraction_mm\": null, \"thermal_treatment\": null, \"feedstock_history\": \"end-of-life\", \"chemistry_resolved\": false, \"classification_evidence\": [\"Materials and Reagents. Spent LIBs from different consumer electronics, including mobile phones, cameras, and portable power storage devices, were collected for this study. The total weight of the samples was 1 kg. Aqua regia (volume ratio of HCl to H V̅O3 = 3: 1) was used to dissolve the cathodic materials fully in order to analyze their metal contents. L-Tartaric acid was employed as the leachant and hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) as the reductant. All the solutions were prepared in deionized water, and all the reagents were of analytical grade.\", \"NCM materials of varying proportions have similar XRD patterns. Moreover, the intensity of peaks in the residues after leaching is clearly weaker than that in the cathodic materials. The content of the metals present in the cathodic materials is shown in Table S1. The metals in the cathodic materials included Li (6.28 wt%), Ni (11.85 wt%), Co (35.52 wt%), Mn (8.15 wt%), and Al (0.11 wt%). The existence of a slight amount of Al is from the already decayed cathode, which was easily shredded during the ultrasonic cleaning.\", \"Analytical Methods. After the sample of cathodic materials was completely dissolved by the aqua regia, the total amounts of Mn, Li, Co, and Ni from the cathodic materials were determined by inductively coupled plasma-atomic emission spectroscopy (ICP-AES, Spectro Arcos FHS12). The concentrations of Mn, Li, Co, and Ni in the leachate were also analyzed using ICP-AES. Leaching efficiency can be calculated using eq 1.\"], \"case_id\": \"source-3bb6879d7ff610d4\", \"paper\": \"Recovery of Lithium, Nickel, Cobalt, and Manganese from Spent Lithium-Ion Batteries Using Scplscp-Tartaric Acid as a Leachant\", \"node_id\": \"http://weee-kg.org/source:Recovery%20of%20Lithium%2C%20Nickel%2C%20Cobalt%2C%20and%20Manganese%20from%20Spent%20Lithium-Ion%20Batteries%20Using%20Scplscp-Tartaric%20Acid%20as%20a%20Leachant:Component_cathodic_material\"}" ;
    analysis:state "Ultrasonically separated cathodic material" ;
    analysis:stream "cathode" .

<urn:ontoweee:analysis:profile:source-bfd8d51d466137b4%2Fblack-powder> a analysis:SampleProfile ;
    analysis:archiveEntityId "Product_lithium_ion_battery" ;
    analysis:case_id "source-bfd8d51d466137b4" ;
    analysis:chemistry "nmc" ;
    analysis:chemistry_resolved true ;
    analysis:evidenceSpan "The spent LIBs powder was analyzed separately by X-ray diffraction (XRD, D/MAX-C). Its diffraction peaks were indexed to carbon and LNCM (LiNi _ x Co _ y Mn _ z O _ 2) as the major phase (Figure 1).",
        "The spent LIBs powder was obtained from a recycling company in Zhejiang, China. The powder is a retired ternary lithium-ion battery that has been processed through “discharge-disassembly-crushingscreening” and other processes to form a black powder. The metal contents of the powders are shown in Table 1." ;
    analysis:feedstock_history "end-of-life" ;
    analysis:id "source-bfd8d51d466137b4/black-powder" ;
    analysis:label "Ternary lithium-ion battery black powder" ;
    analysis:material_class "mixed-electrode-material" ;
    analysis:method_family "Alkaline leaching" ;
    analysis:node_id "http://weee-kg.org/source:Selective%20Leaching%20of%20Valuable%20Metal%20from%20Spent%20Lithium-Ion%20Batteries%20in%20the%20Alkaline%20Glycinate%20System:Product_lithium_ion_battery" ;
    analysis:note "" ;
    analysis:origin "recycling-company" ;
    analysis:paper "Selective Leaching of Valuable Metal from Spent Lithium-Ion Batteries in the Alkaline Glycinate System" ;
