<urn:ontoweee:analysis:profile:nimh-recovery%2Fmixed-scrap> a analysis:SampleProfile ;
    analysis:archiveEntityId "Component_mixed_electrode_scrap" ;
    analysis:case_id "nimh-recovery" ;
    analysis:chemistry "ni-mh" ;
    analysis:chemistry_resolved true ;
    analysis:evidenceSpan "Leach tests on cells were carried out using hydrochloric acid solution. In each test, 3.08 g of battery scrap which consisted of 1.25 g of the positive electrode materials and 1.83 g of the negative electrode materials were leached in 50 ml of HCl solutions of suitable concentrations in a 100-ml beaker under electromagnetic stirring, except for the tests on the effect of solid-to-liquid ratio on the leaching of metals. After leaching, the leach solution and insoluble residue were separated by filtration. The concentrations of each metal in the leach solutions were measured." ;
    analysis:feedstock_history "end-of-life" ;
    analysis:id "nimh-recovery/mixed-scrap" ;
    analysis:label "Ni-MH mixed positive/negative electrode scrap" ;
    analysis:material_class "mixed-electrode-material" ;
    analysis:method_family "Acid leaching" ;
    analysis:node_id "http://weee-kg.org/source:Hydrometallurgical%20Process%20for%20Recovery%20of%20Metal%20Values%20from%20Spent%20Nickel-Metal%20Hydride%20Secondary%20Batteries:Component_mixed_electrode_scrap" ;
    analysis:note "Leaching applies to the mixed electrode feed. Positive and negative compositions remain separate; no mixed-feed concentration is inferred." ;
    analysis:origin "spent-batteries" ;
    analysis:paper "Hydrometallurgical Process for Recovery of Metal Values from Spent Nickel-Metal Hydride Secondary Batteries" ;
    analysis:payload "{\"id\": \"nimh-recovery/mixed-scrap\", \"label\": \"Ni-MH mixed positive/negative electrode scrap\", \"chemistry\": \"ni-mh\", \"stream\": \"mixed-electrodes\", \"origin\": \"spent-batteries\", \"state\": \"1.25 g positive + 1.83 g negative electrode material\", \"entity_ids\": [\"Component_mixed_electrode_scrap\"], \"method_family\": \"Acid leaching\", \"note\": \"Leaching applies to the mixed electrode feed. Positive and negative compositions remain separate; no mixed-feed concentration is inferred.\", \"evidence\": [\"Leach tests on cells were carried out using hydrochloric acid solution. In each test, 3.08 g of battery scrap which consisted of 1.25 g of the positive electrode materials and 1.83 g of the negative electrode materials were leached in 50 ml of HCl solutions of suitable concentrations in a 100-ml beaker under electromagnetic stirring, except for the tests on the effect of solid-to-liquid ratio on the leaching of metals. After leaching, the leach solution and insoluble residue were separated by filtration. The concentrations of each metal in the leach solutions were measured.\"], \"material_class\": \"mixed-electrode-material\", \"preparation\": [\"dismantled\", \"separated\", \"mixed\"], \"size_fraction_mm\": null, \"thermal_treatment\": null, \"feedstock_history\": \"end-of-life\", \"chemistry_resolved\": true, \"classification_evidence\": [\"Leach tests on cells were carried out using hydrochloric acid solution. In each test, 3.08 g of battery scrap which consisted of 1.25 g of the positive electrode materials and 1.83 g of the negative electrode materials were leached in 50 ml of HCl solutions of suitable concentrations in a 100-ml beaker under electromagnetic stirring, except for the tests on the effect of solid-to-liquid ratio on the leaching of metals. After leaching, the leach solution and insoluble residue were separated by filtration. The concentrations of each metal in the leach solutions were measured.\", \"The nickel-metal hydride batteries were cut in half longitudinally to separate the metallic cases from the internal battery rolls which consisted of the positive and negative electrodes plus a nylon separator. The cells weighed an average of 24.8 g each and contained an AB5 - t y p e rare earth metal system negative electrode. The weight of theŽ. positive electrode in one cell was 7.27 g and that of the negative electrode was 10.57 g in which the alloy powder accounted for 87.6% and the metal-mesh 12.4%. The components of the active material on the positive electrode, the alloy powder and the metal-mesh on the negative electrode were determined separately by total dissolution with aqua regia.\"], \"case_id\": \"nimh-recovery\", \"paper\": \"Hydrometallurgical Process for Recovery of Metal Values from Spent Nickel-Metal Hydride Secondary Batteries\", \"node_id\": \"http://weee-kg.org/source:Hydrometallurgical%20Process%20for%20Recovery%20of%20Metal%20Values%20from%20Spent%20Nickel-Metal%20Hydride%20Secondary%20Batteries:Component_mixed_electrode_scrap\"}" ;
    analysis:state "1.25 g positive + 1.83 g negative electrode material" ;
    analysis:stream "mixed-electrodes" .

<urn:ontoweee:analysis:profile:lfp-ultrasound%2Fcathode> a analysis:SampleProfile ;
    analysis:archiveEntityId "Component_cathode_material" ;
    analysis:case_id "lfp-ultrasound" ;
    analysis:chemistry "lfp" ;
    analysis:chemistry_resolved true ;
    analysis:evidenceSpan "Spent LFP batteries were obtained from a company in Shenzhen City, China. To eliminate the risk of short-circuiting and fire during subsequent manual dismantling, the batteries were first immersed in 5 wt% NaCl solution for 24 h to allow complete discharge [25]. The discharged batteries were then manually dismantled to take whole cathode and anode electrodes. The cathode electrodes were cut into pieces measuring 10 mm × 10 mm, followed by vacuum pyrolysis at 450 ^∘C for 2 h to remove the organic binder, thus obtaining cathode materials of spent LFP batteries. The cathode powder was subjected to thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) prior to pyrolysis to determine the appropriate binder removal temperature. The TGA curve of the cathode material showed that the sample mass remained essentially unchanged below 300 ^∘C, beyond which a significant weight-loss event was observed, indicating that effective decomposition of the PVDF binder requires temperatures above 300 ^∘C. PVDF is a semicrystalline fluoropolymer that undergoes thermal decomposition via dehydrofluorination and chain scission predominantly in the range of 350 - 500 ^∘C under inert or vacuum conditions, releasing HF and other gaseous products [26]. A pyrolysis temperature of 450 ^∘C was therefore selected to ensure complete binder removal and reliable detachment of the cathode active material from the aluminum foil current collector, while minimizing energy consumption. The vacuum atmosphere further facilitates the escape of gaseous decomposition products and enhances the overall pretreatment efficiency [27]. These cathode materials were ground and then used as the experimental leaching material. Specimens weighing 25 mg of cathode materials from spent LFP batteries were weighed and dissolved in 10 mL chloroazotic acid to be diluted and reconstructed to 1000 mL. An ICAP-7000 ICP-OES was employed to measure the contents of metals in the cathode materials of spent LFP batteries." ;
    analysis:feedstock_history "end-of-life" ;
    analysis:id "lfp-ultrasound/cathode" ;
    analysis:label "LFP cathode material" ;
    analysis:material_class "cathode-active-material" ;
    analysis:method_family "Acid leaching" ;
    analysis:node_id "http://weee-kg.org/source:Recycling%20of%20Spent%20LiFePO%204%20Batteries%20Using%20Ultrasonic-Assisted%20Reducing%20Leaching:Component_cathode_material" ;
    analysis:note "Conventional and ultrasonic arms remain distinguished by observation conditions. Activation energy and the aggregate Others category are excluded from composition/recovery summaries." ;
    analysis:origin "spent-batteries" ;
    analysis:paper "Recycling of Spent LiFePO 4 Batteries Using Ultrasonic-Assisted Reducing Leaching" ;
    analysis:payload "{\"id\": \"lfp-ultrasound/cathode\", \"label\": \"LFP cathode material\", \"chemistry\": \"lfp\", \"stream\": \"cathode\", \"origin\": \"spent-batteries\", \"state\": \"Dismantled, cut and vacuum-pyrolyzed at 450 °C for 2 h\", \"entity_ids\": [\"Component_cathode_material\"], \"method_family\": \"Acid leaching\", \"note\": \"Conventional and ultrasonic arms remain distinguished by observation conditions. Activation energy and the aggregate Others category are excluded from composition/recovery summaries.\", \"evidence\": [\"Spent LFP batteries were obtained from a company in Shenzhen City, China. To eliminate the risk of short-circuiting and fire during subsequent manual dismantling, the batteries were first immersed in 5 wt% NaCl solution for 24 h to allow complete discharge [25]. The discharged batteries were then manually dismantled to take whole cathode and anode electrodes. The cathode electrodes were cut into pieces measuring 10 mm × 10 mm, followed by vacuum pyrolysis at 450 ^∘C for 2 h to remove the organic binder, thus obtaining cathode materials of spent LFP batteries. The cathode powder was subjected to thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) prior to pyrolysis to determine the appropriate binder removal temperature. The TGA curve of the cathode material showed that the sample mass remained essentially unchanged below 300 ^∘C, beyond which a significant weight-loss event was observed, indicating that effective decomposition of the PVDF binder requires temperatures above 300 ^∘C. PVDF is a semicrystalline fluoropolymer that undergoes thermal decomposition via dehydrofluorination and chain scission predominantly in the range of 350 - 500 ^∘C under inert or vacuum conditions, releasing HF and other gaseous products [26]. A pyrolysis temperature of 450 ^∘C was therefore selected to ensure complete binder removal and reliable detachment of the cathode active material from the aluminum foil current collector, while minimizing energy consumption. The vacuum atmosphere further facilitates the escape of gaseous decomposition products and enhances the overall pretreatment efficiency [27]. These cathode materials were ground and then used as the experimental leaching material. Specimens weighing 25 mg of cathode materials from spent LFP batteries were weighed and dissolved in 10 mL chloroazotic acid to be diluted and reconstructed to 1000 mL. An ICAP-7000 ICP-OES was employed to measure the contents of metals in the cathode materials of spent LFP batteries.\"], \"material_class\": \"cathode-active-material\", \"preparation\": [\"dismantled\", \"cut\", \"vacuum-pyrolyzed\"], \"size_fraction_mm\": null, \"thermal_treatment\": {\"temperature_c\": 450, \"time_min\": 120}, \"feedstock_history\": \"end-of-life\", \"chemistry_resolved\": true, \"classification_evidence\": [\"Spent LFP batteries were obtained from a company in Shenzhen City, China. To eliminate the risk of short-circuiting and fire during subsequent manual dismantling, the batteries were first immersed in 5 wt% NaCl solution for 24 h to allow complete discharge [25]. The discharged batteries were then manually dismantled to take whole cathode and anode electrodes. The cathode electrodes were cut into pieces measuring 10 mm × 10 mm, followed by vacuum pyrolysis at 450 ^∘C for 2 h to remove the organic binder, thus obtaining cathode materials of spent LFP batteries. The cathode powder was subjected to thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) prior to pyrolysis to determine the appropriate binder removal temperature. The TGA curve of the cathode material showed that the sample mass remained essentially unchanged below 300 ^∘C, beyond which a significant weight-loss event was observed, indicating that effective decomposition of the PVDF binder requires temperatures above 300 ^∘C. PVDF is a semicrystalline fluoropolymer that undergoes thermal decomposition via dehydrofluorination and chain scission predominantly in the range of 350 - 500 ^∘C under inert or vacuum conditions, releasing HF and other gaseous products [26]. A pyrolysis temperature of 450 ^∘C was therefore selected to ensure complete binder removal and reliable detachment of the cathode active material from the aluminum foil current collector, while minimizing energy consumption. The vacuum atmosphere further facilitates the escape of gaseous decomposition products and enhances the overall pretreatment efficiency [27]. These cathode materials were ground and then used as the experimental leaching material. Specimens weighing 25 mg of cathode materials from spent LFP batteries were weighed and dissolved in 10 mL chloroazotic acid to be diluted and reconstructed to 1000 mL. An ICAP-7000 ICP-OES was employed to measure the contents of metals in the cathode materials of spent LFP batteries.\"], \"case_id\": \"lfp-ultrasound\", \"paper\": \"Recycling of Spent LiFePO 4 Batteries Using Ultrasonic-Assisted Reducing Leaching\", \"node_id\": \"http://weee-kg.org/source:Recycling%20of%20Spent%20LiFePO%204%20Batteries%20Using%20Ultrasonic-Assisted%20Reducing%20Leaching:Component_cathode_material\"}" ;
    analysis:state "Dismantled, cut and vacuum-pyrolyzed at 450 °C for 2 h" ;
    analysis:stream "cathode" .

