<urn:ontoweee:analysis:profile:ultrasound%2Fcathode> a analysis:SampleProfile ;
    analysis:archiveEntityId "Component_cathode" ;
    analysis:case_id "ultrasound" ;
    analysis:chemistry "lco" ;
    analysis:chemistry_resolved true ;
    analysis:evidenceSpan "The XRD patterns of the raw materials and the leaching residues for conventional leaching and ultrasound-assisted leaching under the optimum conditions, respectively, were presented in between conventional leaching and ultrasound-assisted leaching. The main composition was lithium cobalt oxide, which is the same as raw materials. However, the intensity of peaks for LiCoO2 in diffractogram decreased significantly after the ultrasound-assisted leaching and the conventional leaching. This is mainly due to the incomplete reaction of LiCoO2 and the acid [29].",
        "The spent LIBs used in this work were kindly donated to us by Tianjin Lishen Battery Joint Stock Co. Ltd.. N-Methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP, supplied by Damao Chemical Reagent Factory, China) was used to separate the cathode materials from the aluminum foil. Sulfuric acid (H2SO4 supplied by Tianjin Fengchuan Chemical Reagent Technologies Co., Ltd., China) was used as the leaching agent. Hydrogen peroxide (H2O2,, supplied by Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd., China) was used as reducing agent. All reagents were of analytical grade and used without further purification." ;
    analysis:feedstock_history "end-of-life" ;
    analysis:id "ultrasound/cathode" ;
    analysis:label "Calcined cathode active material" ;
    analysis:material_class "cathode-active-material" ;
    analysis:method_family "Acid leaching" ;
    analysis:node_id "http://weee-kg.org/source:Ultrasound-Assisted%20Leaching%20of%20Cobalt%20and%20Lithium%20from%20Spent%20Lithium-Ion%20Batteries:Component_cathode" ;
    analysis:note "" ;
    analysis:origin "spent-batteries" ;
    analysis:paper "Ultrasound-Assisted Leaching of Cobalt and Lithium from Spent Lithium-Ion Batteries" ;
    analysis:payload "{\"id\": \"ultrasound/cathode\", \"label\": \"Calcined cathode active material\", \"chemistry\": \"lco\", \"stream\": \"cathode\", \"origin\": \"spent-batteries\", \"state\": \"NMP-separated and calcined at 700 °C\", \"entity_ids\": [\"Component_cathode\"], \"method_family\": \"Acid leaching\", \"note\": \"\", \"evidence\": [\"The spent LIBs used in this work were kindly donated to us by Tianjin Lishen Battery Joint Stock Co. Ltd.. N-Methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP, supplied by Damao Chemical Reagent Factory, China) was used to separate the cathode materials from the aluminum foil. Sulfuric acid (H2SO4 supplied by Tianjin Fengchuan Chemical Reagent Technologies Co., Ltd., China) was used as the leaching agent. Hydrogen peroxide (H2O2,, supplied by Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd., China) was used as reducing agent. All reagents were of analytical grade and used without further purification.\", \"The XRD patterns of the raw materials and the leaching residues for conventional leaching and ultrasound-assisted leaching under the optimum conditions, respectively, were presented in between conventional leaching and ultrasound-assisted leaching. The main composition was lithium cobalt oxide, which is the same as raw materials. However, the intensity of peaks for LiCoO2 in diffractogram decreased significantly after the ultrasound-assisted leaching and the conventional leaching. This is mainly due to the incomplete reaction of LiCoO2 and the acid [29].\"], \"material_class\": \"cathode-active-material\", \"preparation\": [\"solvent-separated\", \"calcined\"], \"size_fraction_mm\": null, \"thermal_treatment\": {\"temperature_c\": 700}, \"feedstock_history\": \"end-of-life\", \"chemistry_resolved\": true, \"classification_evidence\": [\"The spent LIBs used in this work were kindly donated to us by Tianjin Lishen Battery Joint Stock Co. Ltd.. N-Methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP, supplied by Damao Chemical Reagent Factory, China) was used to separate the cathode materials from the aluminum foil. Sulfuric acid (H2SO4 supplied by Tianjin Fengchuan Chemical Reagent Technologies Co., Ltd., China) was used as the leaching agent. Hydrogen peroxide (H2O2,, supplied by Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd., China) was used as reducing agent. All reagents were of analytical grade and used without further purification.\", \"The XRD patterns of the raw materials and the leaching residues for conventional leaching and ultrasound-assisted leaching under the optimum conditions, respectively, were presented in between conventional leaching and ultrasound-assisted leaching. The main composition was lithium cobalt oxide, which is the same as raw materials. However, the intensity of peaks for LiCoO2 in diffractogram decreased significantly after the ultrasound-assisted leaching and the conventional leaching. This is mainly due to the incomplete reaction of LiCoO2 and the acid [29].\"], \"case_id\": \"ultrasound\", \"paper\": \"Ultrasound-Assisted Leaching of Cobalt and Lithium from Spent Lithium-Ion Batteries\", \"node_id\": \"http://weee-kg.org/source:Ultrasound-Assisted%20Leaching%20of%20Cobalt%20and%20Lithium%20from%20Spent%20Lithium-Ion%20Batteries:Component_cathode\"}" ;
    analysis:state "NMP-separated and calcined at 700 °C" ;
    analysis:stream "cathode" .

<urn:ontoweee:analysis:profile:battery-bioleaching%2Fpowder> a analysis:SampleProfile ;
    analysis:archiveEntityId "Component_battery_battery_powder",
        "Component_lithium_ion_battery",
        "Component_powder" ;
    analysis:case_id "battery-bioleaching" ;
    analysis:chemistry "mixed-li-ion" ;
    analysis:chemistry_resolved false ;
    analysis:evidenceSpan "Spent mobile phone batteries were collected from mobile phone markets. All batteries were Li-ion made from different manufacturers with different sizes and types of cathode. At first, all spent batteries were discharged to avoid self-ignition and shortcircuiting. Spent batteries were then dismantled and separated manually into cathodes, anodes, plastic separators and metal cases. Afterwards, the classified portions were weighed. Next, cathodes and anodes were dried at 60 ^∘𝖢 for 24 h to remove moisture. After drying, the classified cathodes and anodes were once again weighed so as to calculate the percentage of battery components. The average percentage of different parts of batteries is shown in Fig. 1.",
        "The metal composition of LIB powder was determined by both chemical digestion and submitting to X-ray fluorescence (XRF) and subsequent analysis with XRF analyzer (PW2404, Philips, Netherland). In chemical digestion, the metal contents were determined using four acids, namely HCl, HNO, HClO and HF. At first, 0.5 g of powder was subjected to 5 mL HNO3 in a Teflon beaker and heated to 150 C for 0.5 h. After cooling,10 mL mixture of HClO4 and HF in a ratio of 1:6 was added and heated to 200 ^∘𝖢. After cooling, 20 mL 1:1 HCl was added and heated again for 0.5 h on a hot plate. When the digest was cooled, it was filtered and made up to 200 mL with deionized water, the final dilution factor being 400. Subsequently, the solution was analyzed through the use of an inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometer (ICP-OES) (Vistapro, Varian, Australia). In addition, a control sample was prepared, whose metal concentration was subtracted from the main sample in the calculations. The results are shown in Table 1." ;
    analysis:feedstock_history "end-of-life" ;
    analysis:id "battery-bioleaching/powder" ;
    analysis:label "Mixed cathode and anode powder" ;
    analysis:material_class "mixed-electrode-material" ;
    analysis:method_family "Bioleaching" ;
    analysis:node_id "http://weee-kg.org/source:Bioleaching%20of%20Valuable%20Metals%20from%20Spent%20Lithium-Ion%20Mobile%20Phone%20Batteries%20Using%20Aspergillus%20Niger:Component_powder" ;
    analysis:note "XRF and digestion are assays of the same sample group." ;
    analysis:origin "end-of-life-phones" ;
    analysis:paper "Bioleaching of Valuable Metals from Spent Lithium-Ion Mobile Phone Batteries Using Aspergillus Niger" ;
    analysis:payload "{\"id\": \"battery-bioleaching/powder\", \"label\": \"Mixed cathode and anode powder\", \"chemistry\": \"mixed-li-ion\", \"stream\": \"mixed-electrodes\", \"origin\": \"end-of-life-phones\", \"state\": \"Dried, crushed, milled and sieved below 75 µm\", \"entity_ids\": [\"Component_powder\", \"Component_battery_battery_powder\", \"Component_lithium_ion_battery\"], \"method_family\": \"Bioleaching\", \"note\": \"XRF and digestion are assays of the same sample group.\", \"evidence\": [\"Spent mobile phone batteries were collected from mobile phone markets. All batteries were Li-ion made from different manufacturers with different sizes and types of cathode. At first, all spent batteries were discharged to avoid self-ignition and shortcircuiting. Spent batteries were then dismantled and separated manually into cathodes, anodes, plastic separators and metal cases. Afterwards, the classified portions were weighed. Next, cathodes and anodes were dried at 60 ^∘𝖢 for 24 h to remove moisture. After drying, the classified cathodes and anodes were once again weighed so as to calculate the percentage of battery components. The average percentage of different parts of batteries is shown in Fig. 1.\", \"The metal composition of LIB powder was determined by both chemical digestion and submitting to X-ray fluorescence (XRF) and subsequent analysis with XRF analyzer (PW2404, Philips, Netherland). In chemical digestion, the metal contents were determined using four acids, namely HCl, HNO, HClO and HF. At first, 0.5 g of powder was subjected to 5 mL HNO3 in a Teflon beaker and heated to 150 \\u0005C for 0.5 h. After cooling,10 mL mixture of HClO4 and HF in a ratio of 1:6 was added and heated to 200 ^∘𝖢. After cooling, 20 mL 1:1 HCl was added and heated again for 0.5 h on a hot plate. When the digest was cooled, it was filtered and made up to 200 mL with deionized water, the final dilution factor being 400. Subsequently, the solution was analyzed through the use of an inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometer (ICP-OES) (Vistapro, Varian, Australia). In addition, a control sample was prepared, whose metal concentration was subtracted from the main sample in the calculations. The results are shown in Table 1.\"], \"material_class\": \"mixed-electrode-material\", \"preparation\": [\"dismantled\", \"dried\", \"milled\", \"screened\"], \"size_fraction_mm\": [null, 0.075], \"thermal_treatment\": null, \"feedstock_history\": \"end-of-life\", \"chemistry_resolved\": false, \"classification_evidence\": [\"Spent mobile phone batteries were collected from mobile phone markets. All batteries were Li-ion made from different manufacturers with different sizes and types of cathode. At first, all spent batteries were discharged to avoid self-ignition and shortcircuiting. Spent batteries were then dismantled and separated manually into cathodes, anodes, plastic separators and metal cases. Afterwards, the classified portions were weighed. Next, cathodes and anodes were dried at 60 ^∘𝖢 for 24 h to remove moisture. After drying, the classified cathodes and anodes were once again weighed so as to calculate the percentage of battery components. The average percentage of different parts of batteries is shown in Fig. 1.\", \"The metal composition of LIB powder was determined by both chemical digestion and submitting to X-ray fluorescence (XRF) and subsequent analysis with XRF analyzer (PW2404, Philips, Netherland). In chemical digestion, the metal contents were determined using four acids, namely HCl, HNO, HClO and HF. At first, 0.5 g of powder was subjected to 5 mL HNO3 in a Teflon beaker and heated to 150 \\u0005C for 0.5 h. After cooling,10 mL mixture of HClO4 and HF in a ratio of 1:6 was added and heated to 200 ^∘𝖢. After cooling, 20 mL 1:1 HCl was added and heated again for 0.5 h on a hot plate. When the digest was cooled, it was filtered and made up to 200 mL with deionized water, the final dilution factor being 400. Subsequently, the solution was analyzed through the use of an inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometer (ICP-OES) (Vistapro, Varian, Australia). In addition, a control sample was prepared, whose metal concentration was subtracted from the main sample in the calculations. The results are shown in Table 1.\"], \"case_id\": \"battery-bioleaching\", \"paper\": \"Bioleaching of Valuable Metals from Spent Lithium-Ion Mobile Phone Batteries Using Aspergillus Niger\", \"node_id\": \"http://weee-kg.org/source:Bioleaching%20of%20Valuable%20Metals%20from%20Spent%20Lithium-Ion%20Mobile%20Phone%20Batteries%20Using%20Aspergillus%20Niger:Component_powder\"}" ;
    analysis:state "Dried, crushed, milled and sieved below 75 µm" ;
    analysis:stream "mixed-electrodes" .

<urn:ontoweee:analysis:method:nmc-lactic%2FProcess_lactic_acid_leaching> a analysis:Treatment ;
